  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Write $-12{t}^{2}-108$ in factored form by rewriting the second term of the expression as the square of an imaginary number. Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$-12{t}^{2}-108=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor out $-3$ from the expression:\n\n$$\n-12{t}^{2}-108=-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36).\n$$\n\nNow focus on factoring $(4{t}^{2}+36).$  \n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${i}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor it as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{2}+36 & =4{t}^{2}-(-36) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}-(36{i}^{2}) \\\\  & =(2t)^{2}-(6i)^{2} \\\\  & =(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3\\,(4{t}^{2}+36)=-3(2t+6i)(2t-6i).\n$$",
